WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.840
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.300
SPEAKER: Welcome to the
Google Cloud Marketplace

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.500
Private Catalog.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.980
The Private Catalog is a service
on Google Cloud allowing Cloud

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.500
admins to control
distribution of solutions

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.630
to ensure compliance
and governance,

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.610
and make solutions
easily discoverable

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.390
within their organization.

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:21.390
So what does that really mean?

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.490
An admin at a company
wants to ensure

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.440
that the platform's
group has access

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.420
to a set of Cloud solutions.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.730
Some solutions are
custom to the company,

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.700
and others are solutions they've
purchased on Google Cloud.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.370
And they need to be
curated by region, by team,

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:38.400
and different criteria
your organization needs.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.430
The way they do this is
creating a catalog instance,

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.850
add solutions to it, and share
it with a group of users.

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:47.220
Not only is it shared,
Private Catalog

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:50.220
can ensure that the solution is
configured and ready to deploy

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.250
or use all in one place.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:56.130
Cloud admins can manage a
central place for end users

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.890
to find approved solutions.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:01.710
Admins have the ability to
push solutions with the latest

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.210
patches or versions.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:06.060
End users don't have to worry
about what's being deployed,

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:07.800
and can be assured
that they're always

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.660
using the latest
and greatest version

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.420
of every approved solution.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:15.630
Admins can use templates to
add parameters for users.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:18.120
They can place limits on
who can deploy resources,

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.910
and which resources
they can deploy.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:23.130
And finally, we've
made it easy for admins

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:26.160
to take third-party solutions
from Google Cloud Marketplace

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.340
and add them to their
private catalog.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.560
We're going to explore
the Admin view first,

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.300
which right now, is
in the zero state

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.550
with no catalogs or solutions.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:37.200
We can get started
on changing that

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.110
by clicking Go to
Catalog Manager or Manage

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:43.820
Solutions on the left
side navigation bar.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.200
Let's go ahead and
create a new solution.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.210
Start by clicking solutions
in the left navigation bar,

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:51.430
and then create solution.

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:54.190
The Private Catalog supports
three solution types,

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:57.940
Terraform configs, Links,
and Deployment Manager.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:00.310
Terraform is a tool
for building, changing

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:02.650
and versioning Cloud
Infrastructure safely

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:03.940
and efficiently.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:07.360
Terraform configs are a way to
deploy first-party Google Cloud

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.150
Services, but using Terraform.

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:12.790
Links solutions are
really just a URL.

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:15.220
This could be to
documentation or whatever it

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:16.900
is that you need to point to.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:20.110
Links work for SAS
solutions, such as a pointer

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:24.070
to MongoDB's product offering
on Google Cloud marketplace.

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:26.020
Deployment Manager
solutions are used

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:29.110
to deploy any Google Cloud
first-party service, including

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:32.470
storage, VMs,
containers or BigQuery.

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:35.890
For this example, we'll
select Terraform config.

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:38.530
A form will appear on the
right side of the screen.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:41.350
The top six fields are
consistent across all solution

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:43.720
types, starting with
name and description,

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.240
and also including a
tagline for the Browse page

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:50.650
of the Private Catalog, an icon,
support information, and a link

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:52.420
to any relevant documentation.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:54.272
[CLICKING]

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:58.530
After that, we need to add a
link to the Terraform config.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:00.780
This one is called
Google Cloud Storage,

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.870
and it points to a zip
file in a GCS bucket,

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:07.800
gs://terraform
samples/gcs bucket.zip.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:11.310
The zip file should include
your Terraform configs.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:13.870
Once we've attached the
zip file, hit Create.

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:17.520
And this adds the solution to
the Private Catalog, a process

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:18.882
that takes a couple seconds.

00:03:18.882 --> 00:03:21.090
If you're wondering what's
happening in the back end,

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:24.750
the system is leveraging Cloud
Build to run Terraform validate

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:27.960
and parse out the input
definitions for the product.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.550
While that's
happening, the solution

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:30.960
will appear in
the table, and you

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.970
can click on it to review
the information that

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.560
was added earlier.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:36.990
Validation status
will remain as pending

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:39.840
while the solution is being
processed, after which,

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.670
it should change to valid.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:45.300
Let's add a send grade
email API link solution.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.970
Again, click solutions
in the left navigation

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:52.894
bar, followed by create solution
in the bar above the table.

00:03:52.894 --> 00:03:55.314
[CLICKING]

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:09.450
You can also add the
Deployment Manager solution.

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:12.540
Click Create Solution in
the bar above the table.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:14.535
Enter basic metadata
for the solution.

00:04:14.535 --> 00:04:16.176
[CLICKING]

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:22.460
And attach a zip file
for the solution.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:24.650
This WordPress Deployment
Manager template

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:27.050
was downloaded from the
WordPress solution page,

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:29.570
available in the Marketplace.

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:31.670
Now let's create a catalog.

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:35.330
First, head to catalogs using
the left navigation bar.

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:37.760
A catalog is really just
a grouping of products.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:40.250
And the way to make your
products available to end users

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:43.290
is by creating a catalog,
adding solutions to it,

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.140
and then sharing that
catalog with a part

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:47.780
of your organization's
Google Cloud hierarchy.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:50.330
When creating catalogs, you
may want to group solutions

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:53.810
by region, by disciplines,
like development or testing,

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:56.360
or really whatever makes
sense for your company.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.250
You can create as many
catalogs as you need.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.250
Hit the Create catalog button.

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:05.090
The form prompts us to add a
catalog ID and a description.

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:08.630
This catalog is called DevTools,
with the descriptor development

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:09.860
tools.

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:12.950
Click Create to finish
setting up the catalog.

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:16.190
The new catalog appears
in the catalogs table.

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:17.660
Now that we've
created a catalog,

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:19.370
we can assign it a solution.

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:21.800
Click into the catalog
we've just created.

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:25.460
In the Details page,
click on Assign Solutions.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:27.470
Find the Google Cloud
Storage solution

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:30.710
we created earlier
and press assign.

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:33.500
Let's go back to the catalogs
page using the Back button

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:36.210
at the top, and add
one more catalog

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:38.180
so we can see how
they work together.

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:42.260
We can name this one Test Tools
with the descriptor testing

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:44.670
tools.

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:46.600
Once the testing
catalog is saved,

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:49.730
we can assign the
link solution to it.

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:52.730
Now that we have two catalogs,
each containing solutions,

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:55.670
it's time to share them with the
right users in the Google Cloud

00:05:55.670 --> 00:05:56.900
hierarchy.

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:59.150
Head back to catalogs
page and select

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:00.800
the two catalogs we've created.

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:03.450
Go to Share from the
options at the top.

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:06.080
And from here, you can
choose to share the catalog

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:09.200
at the organization
folder or project level.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:11.870
We're going to share both
Catalogs at the project level

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:14.510
with a project called
Product Manager.

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:16.430
Everyone in the
Google Cloud hierarchy

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:18.320
with access to that
project will now

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:21.800
see and have access to the
solutions within the catalog.

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:24.650
We are now going to click
Browse Catalog on the top right

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:28.250
side of the screen to take
a look at the end user view.

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:31.430
Let's go ahead and filter by
the catalog we created earlier,

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:32.810
DevTools.

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:36.260
This brings up our Terraform
config, Google Cloud Storage.

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:38.810
Clicking on the solution
loads the Details page,

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:41.810
a page that is similar across
all three types of solutions

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:43.430
in the Private Catalog.

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:45.860
This makes it easy for
end users to quickly find

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:47.600
solutions they can use.

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:50.690
Now let's deploy our
Terraform config.

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:53.690
Click Deploy to begin
the new deployment.

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:55.760
Here we will fill out
the input variables

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:57.620
needed for the deployment.

00:06:57.620 --> 00:06:59.930
For Terraform configs,
we also have something

00:06:59.930 --> 00:07:01.490
like a pre-flight check.

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:03.560
When we click
Preview and Deploy,

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:05.840
the system will start
to run Terraform plan

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:07.100
in the background.

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:09.770
This happens without having
to install Terraform, get

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:11.690
the binaries or anything else.

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:14.570
These services are abstracted
from you and the end user,

00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:17.910
allowing you to easily deploy
the solutions you need.

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:20.910
In this case, Terraform
plan has run successfully.

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:23.400
So we can go ahead and
deploy the solution.

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:26.300
In this case, we will
create a GCS bucket.

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:28.580
Once the deployment has
successfully completed,

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:30.350
the icon turns green.

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:32.030
This usually takes
a few minutes,

00:07:32.030 --> 00:07:35.630
but it does depend on the
solution you are deploying.

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:37.400
Let's take a look
at a situation where

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:39.020
the deployment has failed.

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:40.850
Clicking the deployment
shows the logs,

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:44.210
enabling the user to potentially
self-service and fix the issue.

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:49.060
With our solution
successfully deployed,

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:51.240
we can go back to the
Private Catalog homepage

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:53.790
and browse all Terraform
deployments using the tab

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:55.950
in the left navigation bar.

00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:58.800
The Private Catalog gives
organizations a single place

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:01.560
to discover approved solutions
that they can deploy,

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:03.900
knowing the solutions have
been approved and verified

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:05.700
by their Cloud admin.

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:08.640
It also looks a lot like the
Google Cloud marketplace,

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:10.120
and for a reason.

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:12.090
We've aimed to create
a consistent UI

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:14.520
experience to help you
and your users easily

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:17.790
navigate and enjoy the benefits
of the Private Catalog.

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:19.140
Ready to get going?

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:22.470
Begin building on Google Cloud
with $300 in free credits

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:24.720
and 20-plus always
free products.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:26.840
Get started today.

